cs_content_header = cs_long_name
</python>

This page is designed to help you get an instance of CAT-SOOP up and running.
If you are setting things up on a local copy, following the instructions on
this page should be enough.  If you are setting up a public-facing instance on
a server you control, you should follow these instructions, and then continue
by following the link at the bottom of this page for more information about
server configuration.

As a general rule, catsoop is only ever really "officially" tested using Debian
GNU/Linux, but others have tested these instructions on Mac OSX and on Windows
(both WSL and Cygwin).

<tableofcontents/>

<catsoop-section>Install Necessary Software</catsoop-section>

CAT-SOOP depends on Python (version 3.11+, with pip and venv).

You will need Python version 3.11+ on your system to run CAT-SOOP.  I'm sure
that almost all distributions have Python 3.11+ in their package managers,
though it may be necessary to download the source from [the official Python
site](https://www.python.org/).

On Debian Bookworm, you will need the `python3`, `python3-pip`, and
`python3-venv` packages (or a version of Python 3.11+ installed in some other
way).

<catsoop-section>Download CAT-SOOP</catsoop-section>

There are several ways you can install CAT-SOOP.

<catsoop-subsection>Installation via pip</catsoop-subsection>

The easiest is pip, with a command like the following (for the most recent
version):

```
$ pip3 install catsoop
```

!!! WARNING: Virtual Environments
    We recommend installing catsoop in a virtual environment.  If you want
    to install it to your system Python instead, you may need to run the above
    commands as root or use `sudo`.

<catsoop-subsection>Manual Installation</catsoop-subsection>

Alternatively, you can clone the repository with Git:

```
$ git clone https://catsoop.org/git/catsoop/catsoop
```

After you have a local copy, you can then run:

```
$ make install
```

from the source directory.  You can also run the unit tests by running:

```
$ make test
```


<catsoop-section>Configure CAT-SOOP</catsoop-section>

To configure CAT-SOOP, run the following command:

```
catsoop configure
```

and answer the questions it poses.

!!! note: Note
    If you are running a public-facing CAT-SOOP instance, you are **strongly
    encouraged** to enable encryption if the directory in which you are storing
    the logs is not already encrypted in some way (e.g., via `luks` or
    `gocryptfs` or `cryfs`, etc).

<catsoop-section>Add Courses</catsoop-section>

The default location for CAT-SOOP courses is in
`~/.local/share/catsoop/courses/`.  You should move (or symlink) your courses
to that location.

If you're not sure what to put there, a decent place to start is with the
"sample course", which you can put in the appropriate place with a command like
the following, using Git:

```
$ git clone  https://catsoop.org/git/catsoop/sample_course ~/.local/share/catsoop/courses/sample_course
```

<catsoop-section>Configure Syntax Highlighting</catsoop-section>

Syntax highlighting is available for a few common editors:

* For [Vim](https://www.vim.org/), copy the contents of the [`syntax-highlighting/vim`](https://catsoop.org/git/catsoop/catsoop/src/branch/main/syntax-highlighting/vim) directory in the catsoop repository to your `~/.vim` directory.
* For [Emacs](https://www.gnu.org/software/emacs/), follow the instructions in [`syntax-highlighting/emacs/README.md`](https://catsoop.org/git/catsoop/catsoop/src/branch/main/syntax-highlighting/emacs).
* For [VSCodium](https://vscodium.com/)/VSCode, you can either [download the catsoop plugin](COURSE/catsoop-syntax-0.0.1.vsix) or build it yourself using [these instructions](https://catsoop.org/git/catsoop/catsoop/src/branch/main/syntax-highlighting/vscode), and then either:
  * click-and-drag that file into the "extensions" panel of your editor window,
  * under the extensions panel, choose "Install from VSIX" and navigate to the `catsoop-syntax-0.0.1.vsix` file, or
  * run `code --install-extension catsoop-syntax-0.0.1.vsix` from a terminal window.

<catsoop-section>Start CAT-SOOP</catsoop-section>

To start the server, you should run:

```
$ catsoop start
```

This will start the server listening on port 7667.  You should then be able to
directy your browser to `http://localhost:7667` to see the CAT-SOOP instance.


<catsoop-section>Additional Configuration for Public-Facing Servers</catsoop-section>

If you are setting up a public-facing CAT-SOOP instance, see [this page about
additional server configuration](CURRENT/server_configuration) as well.
